for exp-001 through exp-004, exp-007 through exp-012, and the ones currently preparing like exp-014 and exp-015, while keeping the completed ones separate since they already have actual results. I need to clarify which experiments actually need generated data. The ones marked DONE in the registry already have real data, so I should focus on generating detailed data for the incomplete experiments—those that are either not yet in the registry or still in PREPARING status.\n\nNow I'm working through the median tier values for each experiment, starting with how the 32B model variants should compare to the 8B baseline. For the cross-architecture static probe experiment, I'm estimating that the crossing rate should fall in the 0.35-0.55 range, with the best layer around the 44% depth mark for a 64-layer model, and accuracy slightly lower than the 8B version due to architectural differences.\n\nFor the temporal window experiment, I'm projecting the crossing rate to land between 0.55-0.72, with lead times staying in the -4 to 0 range. The cross-model transfer shows significant degradation in coverage retention around 0.35-0.55, particularly for Llama-to-Qwen and Qwen-to-Llama transfers. Lead time patterns across the 32B models appear weaker than the 8B baseline, with mean lead times around +1-3 but still centering at 0 for the median tier.\n\nI'm also noting that window size shows diminishing returns with an optimal range around 15-20, middle layers perform best though the plateau broadens on 64-layer models, and I'm still working through the 16-label taxonomy results.\n\nNow I'm looking at the remaining experiments—the oracle decomposition analysis, OpenThinker-7B confirming the scale pattern similar to r1-8b, and GRU performance being weaker on 32B models. The BOCPD approach shows lead times in the 2-8 range with positive lead rates around 0.35-0.55, while FPR metrics cluster between 0.1-0.25. I'm ready to compile these into the full YAML output.","signature":""}],"stop_r