{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TX94kEjP4m3LWqQVL1yB19","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: \"Claim 1 holds but needs qualifier ('on most architectures tested'). Claim 2 softens from 'saturates oracle' to 'substantially improves over static' — coverage advantage is real but model-dependent (+15-30pp range). Claim 3 unchanged (GRU/BOCPD both achieve positive mean but unreliable). Cross-architecture story becomes 'phenomenon is consistent but effect magnitudes vary.' Drop 'cross-model transfer' as a claim, report as ablation. FPR discussion added: precision-recall curves show usable operating points exist but require threshold tuning. Reframe contribution as 'first systematic evaluation revealing what does/doesn't transfer.' Submit EMNLP Main but prepare for Findings redirect.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Cross-Architecture Static Probe (32B Models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: \"Layer sweep + static LogisticRegression probe on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B (64 layers, 5120-d hidden states). Identifies best layer, evaluates static detection coverage.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_best_layer_idx: 29\n      r1_32b_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.453\n      r1_32b_best_acc: 0.793\n      r1_32b_best_f1: 0.612\n      r1_32b_static_crossing_rate: 0.42\n      r1_32b_static_lead_mean: -3.1\n      r1_32b_fisher_orig: 14.8\n      r1_32b_fisher_pca3: 0.87\n      r1_32b_var_explained_3pc: 0.142\n      qwq_32b_best_layer_idx: 31\n      qwq_32b_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.484\n      qwq_32b_best_acc: 0.781\n      qwq_32b_best_f1: 0.589\n      qwq_32b_static_crossing_rate: 0.38\n      qwq_32b_static_lead_mean: -4.2\n      qwq_32b_fisher_orig: 13.2\n      qwq_32b_fisher_pca3: 0.81\n      qwq_32b_var_explained_3pc: 0.131\n    result_rationale: \"32B models show the same qualitative pattern as r1-8b — high Fisher discriminant in full space but collapse under PCA — confirming that safety signal exists but is distributed across minor principal components. However, the 5120-d space is sparser relative to training data (same ~209 traces, higher dimensionality), so static LR accuracy drops ~2-4pp. Crossing rates land at 0.38-0.42, above the trivial floor but well below temporal methods, because static probes still cannot capture the temporal density-decline signature. Best layers cluster around 45% depth (layers 29-31), consistent with r1-8b finding that mid-layers encode safety state better than final layers which are optimized for next-token prediction. QwQ-32B performs slightly worse than r1-32b because it uses a different reasoning training recipe (reinforcement learning vs distillation), potentially distributing safety reasoning signal differently across layers.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Cross-Architecture Layer Sweep Summary\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Best Layer\", \"Depth %\", \"Acc\", \"F1\", \"Crossing Rate\", \"Lead Mean\", \"Fisher (orig)\", \"Fisher (PCA-3)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"14\", \"43.8%\", \"0.817\", \"0.646\", \"0.465\", \"-2.0\", \"17.9\", \"0.93\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (Qwen)\", \"29\", \"45.3%\", \"0.793\", \"0.612\", \"0.42\", \"-3.1\", \"14.8\", \"0.87\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\", \"31\", \"48.4%\", \"0.781\", \"0.589\", \"0.38\", \"-4.2\", \"13.2\", \"0.81\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3a: Layer sweep accuracy heatmap (64 layers × 2 models). Clear peak in layers 27-33 for both 32B models, broader plateau than r1-8b's sharp peak at layer 14.\"\n      - \"Fig 3b: Fisher discriminant ratio per layer (original vs PCA-3) for all 3 models. All show 15-20× collapse from full-space to PCA-3, confirming minor-component signal distribution is architecture-independent.\"\n    analysis: \"Static probes on 32B confirm Claim 1 generalizes: detection coverage remains below 50% regardless of architecture. The ~5pp drop in crossing rate (0.465→0.38-0.42) reflects the curse of dimensionality — 5120-d hidden states with only ~200 training traces create a harder linear separation problem. Fisher ratios are slightly lower (13-15 vs 17-18) suggesting safety signal is somewhat more diffuse in larger models, possibly because 32B models have more capacity to entangle safety reasoning with other computations. Key finding: best-layer depth percentage is remarkably consistent (44-48%) across 32-layer and 64-layer architectures, suggesting a universal 'safety encoding depth' in reasoning models.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cross-Architecture Temporal Window Probe (32B Models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: \"Temporal LR with W=15 on r1-32b and QwQ-32B at their respective best layers. Validates whether +30pp coverage improvement from temporal context holds cross-architecture.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.821\n      r1_32b_temporal_w15_crossing_rate: 0.67\n      r1_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean: -1.8\n      r1_32b_temporal_delta_over_static: 0.25\n      qwq_32b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.804\n      qwq_32b_temporal_w15_crossing_rate: 0.58\n      qwq_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean: -3.2\n      qwq_32b_temporal_delta_over_static: 0.20\n      cross_arch_mean_temporal_crossing: 0.625\n      cross_arch_mean_temporal_lead: -2.5\n    result_rationale: \"Temporal context improves coverage on 32B models (+20-25pp over static), but the gain is weaker than r1-8b's +30pp. This attenuation occurs because: (1) 32B models have longer reasoning traces (median ~80 steps vs ~55 for r1-8b), so the W=15 window captures a smaller fraction of the relevant temporal context; (2) safety reasoning density decline patterns are noisier in 32B models — these models interleave safety and task reasoning more fluidly, so the 'clean transition' that W=15 captures on r1-8b is spread across a wider window. r1-32b outperforms QwQ-32B because as a distilled model it more closely mirrors the teacher's reasoning structure, while QwQ's RL-trained reasoning is more variable. The coverage improvement is real (+20-25pp) but model-dependent, requiring Claim 2 to be qualified with 'on most architectures tested, with effect size varying by model.'\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Temporal Window LR (W=15) Cross-Architecture\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Static Crossing\", \"Temporal Crossing\", \"Delta\", \"Temporal Lead Mean\", \"Temporal Acc\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"0.465\", \"0.767\", \"+0.302\", \"-1.1\", \"0.848\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (Qwen)\", \"0.42\", \"0.67\", \"+0.25\", \"-1.8\", \"0.821\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\", \"0.38\", \"0.58\", \"+0.20\", \"-3.2\", \"0.804\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 4a: Bar chart showing static vs temporal crossing rate per model. All models show improvement; error bars (bootstrap 95% CI) overlap between r1-32b and QwQ but both are clearly above their static baselines.\"\n      - \"Fig 4b: Detection delay CDF per model. r1-8b CDF rises fastest (best detection timing), r1-32b intermediate, QwQ-32B has heaviest right tail.\"\n    analysis: \"The temporal advantage is robust (consistently +20-30pp) but not uniform. The oracle ceiling analysis suggests r1-8b's 76.7% may already be near-oracle for that model, while 32B models have higher oracle ceilings (longer traces = more opportunity) that temporal LR doesn't fully exploit. This motivates the window size ablation (exp-007) — W=15 may be suboptimal for 32B models with longer traces. For the paper narrative, Claim 2 becomes: 'Temporal context substantially improves detection coverage across architectures (+20-30pp), though the magnitude is model-dependent and may not reach oracle ceiling on all models.'\"\n\n  - name: \"Cross-Model Transfer\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: \"Zero-shot transfer: train on r1-8b (Llama, 4096-d), test on 32B models (Qwen, 5120-d) via learned projection head. Bidirectional: also train on Qwen models, test on Llama.\"\n    expected_results:\n      llama_to_qwen_r1_32b_crossing_retention: 0.48\n      llama_to_qwen_r1_32b_acc: 0.692\n      llama_to_qwen_qwq_crossing_retention: 0.41\n      llama_to_qwen_qwq_acc: 0.671\n      qwen_to_llama_crossing_retention: 0.52\n      qwen_to_llama_acc: 0.714\n      mean_cross_transfer_retention: 0.47\n      projection_head_dim: \"4096→5120 linear + ReLU + 5120\"\n      within_family_transfer_qwen32_to_qwq: 0.78\n      within_family_transfer_r1_8b_self_cv: 0.95\n    result_rationale: \"Cross-architecture transfer retains only ~40-50% of within-model coverage. The degradation is expected because: (1) hidden-state representations are not aligned across architectures — even same-family models (Qwen-based r1-32b vs QwQ) have different training objectives that shape representation geometry differently; (2) the linear projection head (4096→5120) cannot learn the nonlinear manifold alignment needed for full transfer; (3) safety signal is encoded in architecture-specific minor principal components — the specific directions that carry safety information differ across models. Within-family transfer (Qwen→Qwen) retains ~78%, confirming that shared pre-training creates partially aligned safety representations. The asymmetry (Qwen→Llama slightly better than Llama→Qwen) likely reflects that 32B models have richer representations that project down more gracefully than 8B representations project up. This result means cross-model transfer should be reported as an ablation, not a claim.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Cross-Model Transfer Matrix (Temporal LR W=15, Crossing Rate)\"\n        columns: [\"Train →\", \"Test: r1-8b\", \"Test: r1-32b\", \"Test: QwQ-32B\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"0.767 (self)\", \"0.32\", \"0.27\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (Qwen)\", \"0.35\", \"0.67 (self)\", \"0.52\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\", \"0.31\", \"0.48\", \"0.58 (self)\"]\n      - name: \"Table 5b: Transfer Retention Rate\"\n        columns: [\"Direction\", \"Retention %\", \"Acc Drop\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Llama→Qwen (r1-32b)\", \"48%\", \"-12.9pp\"]\n          - [\"Llama→Qwen (QwQ)\", \"41%\", \"-14.6pp\"]\n          - [\"Qwen→Llama\", \"52%\", \"-10.3pp\"]\n          - [\"Within-Qwen (r1-32b→QwQ)\", \"78%\", \"-4.1pp\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 5: Transfer matrix heatmap. Diagonal (within-model) is brightest. Within-family (Qwen-Qwen) is intermediate. Cross-family cells are darkest, showing 50%+ degradation.\"\n    analysis: \"Cross-model transfer is too unreliable (<50% retention cross-family) to present as a paper claim. However, within-family retention of 78% suggests that pre-training alignment partially preserves safety signal geometry. For the paper, this becomes an ablation in Section 5 with the takeaway: 'Safety commitment representations are model-specific; zero-shot transfer requires architecture-aware alignment that simple projection cannot provide.' This motivates future work on universal safety probes but does not undermine the per-model results.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cross-Architecture Lead Time Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: \"GRU sequence model (256-d, 7-layer input) on 32B models. Report lead time distribution and compare to r1-8b's +4.7 mean.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_mean: 1.8\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_median: 0\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_std: 11.2\n      r1_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.38\n      r1_32b_gru_crossing_rate: 0.62\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean: 0.4\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_median: -1\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_std: 14.7\n      qwq_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.31\n      qwq_32b_gru_crossing_rate: 0.55\n      cross_arch_mean_lead: 1.1\n      cross_arch_positive_lead_rate: 0.345\n    result_rationale: \"GRU lead time degrades substantially on 32B models (+1.8 and +0.4 vs +4.7 on r1-8b). This happens because: (1) 32B models have longer, more complex reasoning traces where the commitment point is less temporally predictable — the GRU must learn longer-range dependencies; (2) with the same ~200 training traces but higher-dimensional input (5120-d × 7 layers = 35840-d concatenated), the GRU is severely underfitting relative to the signal complexity; (3) QwQ-32B's RL-trained reasoning produces more variable commitment dynamics (sometimes abrupt, sometimes gradual), making sequential prediction harder. The median remains 0 or negative, confirming that pre-textual detection is unreliable across architectures — this is not an r1-8b artifact but a fundamental difficulty. Claim 3 narrative holds: positive mean lead exists but is architecture-dependent and unreliable.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: GRU Lead Time Distribution Across Architectures\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Lead Mean\", \"Lead Median\", \"Lead Std\", \"Pos Lead Rate\", \"Crossing Rate\", \"N Detected\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"+4.7\", \"0\", \"13.0\", \"39% (13/33)\", \"0.767\", \"33\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (Qwen)\", \"+1.8\", \"0\", \"11.2\", \"38% (11/29)\", \"0.62\", \"29\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\", \"+0.4\", \"-1\", \"14.7\", \"31% (8/26)\", \"0.55\", \"26\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 6a: Lead time distribution (violin plot) per model. All three show wide spread centered near 0, with r1-8b having the longest right tail.\"\n      - \"Fig 6b: CDF of lead time. Oracle (dashed) achieves 100% positive lead. All GRU curves cross 50% near 0, showing median-zero pattern is consistent.\"\n    analysis: \"The cross-architecture lead time results strengthen the 'fundamental limitation' narrative of Claim 3. Mean lead time is positive on all models but the effect attenuates with model scale (4.7→1.8→0.4), suggesting larger models encode commitment decisions in ways that are harder to anticipate from hidden-state sequences. This is consistent with the hypothesis that 32B models have more 'internal deliberation' before commitment — the hidden states don't leak the decision as early. For the paper, we report this as: 'Pre-textual detection signal exists across architectures (positive mean lead) but is weak and model-dependent, with no model achieving reliable (>50%) early detection.'\"\n\n  - name: \"Window Size Ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: \"Temporal LR with varying window sizes on all 3 models. Finds optimal W and characterizes diminishing returns.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_w1_crossing: 0.465\n      r1_8b_w3_crossing: 0.535\n      r1_8b_w5_crossing: 0.651\n      r1_8b_w10_crossing: 0.721\n      r1_8b_w15_crossing: 0.767\n      r1_8b_w20_crossing: 0.767\n      r1_8b_w25_crossing: 0.744\n      r1_32b_w1_crossing: 0.42\n      r1_32b_w5_crossing: 0.51\n      r1_32b_w10_crossing: 0.60\n      r1_32b_w15_crossing: 0.67\n      r1_32b_w20_crossing: 0.70\n      r1_32b_w25_crossing: 0.69\n      qwq_32b_w1_crossing: 0.38\n      qwq_32b_w5_crossing: 0.46\n      qwq_32b_w10_crossing: 0.54\n      qwq_32b_w15_crossing: 0.58\n      qwq_32b_w20_crossing: 0.61\n      qwq_32b_w25_crossing: 0.60\n      optimal_w_r1_8b: 15\n      optimal_w_r1_32b: 20\n      optimal_w_qwq: 20\n    result_rationale: \"Diminishing returns consistently appear around W=15-20, but 32B models benefit from slightly larger windows (optimal W=20 vs W=15 for r1-8b). This makes physical sense: 32B models have longer reasoning traces (~80 steps median vs ~55), so the 'safety reasoning density decline' pattern that temporal LR captures spans more steps. W=25 shows slight degradation due to overfitting (more parameters with same training data) and dilution of the relevant temporal signal with distant-past information. The W=1 baseline equals static performance (as expected — it's mathematically identical), confirming the improvement is genuinely from temporal context, not hyperparameter differences. The steepest improvement is W=1→W=5 on all models, suggesting the critical signal is in the recent 5-step history (current safety state), with W=5→W=15 adding the trend information.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A1: Window Size × Model Crossing Rate\"\n        columns: [\"W\", \"r1-8b\", \"r1-32b\", \"QwQ-32B\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"1\", \"0.465\", \"0.42\", \"0.38\"]\n          - [\"3\", \"0.535\", \"0.47\", \"0.42\"]\n          - [\"5\", \"0.651\", \"0.51\", \"0.46\"]\n          - [\"10\", \"0.721\", \"0.60\", \"0.54\"]\n          - [\"15\", \"0.767\", \"0.67\", \"0.58\"]\n          - [\"20\", \"0.767\", \"0.70\", \"0.61\"]\n          - [\"25\", \"0.744\", \"0.69\", \"0.60\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A1: Line plot of crossing rate vs window size, one curve per model. All show monotonic increase saturating around W=15-20, with slight decline at W=25. r1-8b curve is highest throughout.\"\n    analysis: \"Supports the paper's claim that temporal context is the key ingredient, not model complexity. The saturation at W=15-20 is interpretable: commitment points follow a period of declining safety reasoning that unfolds over roughly 10-20 reasoning steps. This connects to the oracle's +31-step mean lead — the relevant history window is shorter than the oracle's full lookahead, explaining why temporal LR captures coverage but not lead time.\"\n\n  - name: \"Layer Selection Ablation for Temporal Probe (32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: \"Temporal LR W=15 evaluated at all 64 layers (or representative subset) for both 32B models. Confirms middle-layer finding generalizes.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_best_temporal_layer: 30\n      r1_32b_layer_range_within_1pp: \"26-35\"\n      r1_32b_early_layers_avg_crossing: 0.31\n      r1_32b_mid_layers_avg_crossing: 0.64\n      r1_32b_late_layers_avg_crossing: 0.48\n      qwq_32b_best_temporal_layer: 33\n      qwq_32b_layer_range_within_1pp: \"28-37\"\n      qwq_32b_early_layers_avg_crossing: 0.28\n      qwq_32b_mid_layers_avg_crossing: 0.56\n      qwq_32b_late_layers_avg_crossing: 0.42\n    result_rationale: \"Middle layers (40-55% depth) dominate for temporal probing on 32B models, extending the r1-8b finding (layer 14/32 = 44%). This is consistent with the representation engineering literature — middle layers encode abstract semantic features while later layers are increasingly specialized for output distribution. The 'plateau' (layers within 1pp of best) is broader on 64-layer models (~10 layers vs ~5 for r1-8b's 32 layers), suggesting redundancy in safety encoding at larger scales. Late layers perform worse because they've begun specializing for next-token prediction, compressing the abstract safety-state signal. Early layers perform worst because safety reasoning hasn't yet been processed into a separable representation.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A2: Layer Depth vs Temporal Probe Performance\"\n        columns: [\"Depth Range\", \"r1-32b Crossing\", \"QwQ Crossing\", \"r1-8b (reference)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"0-25% (layers 1-16)\", \"0.31\", \"0.28\", \"0.34\"]\n          - [\"25-50% (layers 17-32)\", \"0.64\", \"0.53\", \"0.72*\"]\n          - [\"50-75% (layers 33-48)\", \"0.57\", \"0.56\", \"0.58\"]\n          - [\"75-100% (layers 49-64)\", \"0.48\", \"0.42\", \"0.41\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A2: Layer index vs crossing rate, color-coded by model. Clear inverted-U shape with peak at 40-55% depth on all models.\"\n    analysis: \"The middle-layer advantage generalizes robustly. For the paper, this means layer selection is not a critical hyperparameter — any layer in the 40-55% depth range performs near-optimally. This is practically important: practitioners don't need expensive layer sweeps, they can use depth-percentage heuristic. Included as supplementary material.\"\n\n  - name: \"16-Label Taxonomy Supervision\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: \"Train temporal LR using fine-grained safety labels (4 types × safe/unsafe × reasoning/action) instead of binary commitment label. Test if richer supervision improves coverage or timing.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_16label_crossing_rate: 0.791\n      r1_8b_16label_lead_mean: -0.3\n      r1_8b_16label_acc: 0.862\n      r1_8b_binary_crossing_rate_ref: 0.767\n      r1_8b_16label_improvement_crossing: 0.024\n      r1_8b_16label_improvement_lead: 0.8\n      r1_32b_16label_crossing_rate: 0.70\n      r1_32b_16label_lead_mean: -1.2\n    result_rationale: \"16-label supervision provides marginal improvement (+2-3pp crossing rate, ~+1 step lead time) over binary. The gain is small because: (1) the binary commitment label already captures the critical transition (safety reasoning ceases), and finer-grained types add noise rather than signal for this specific detection task; (2) the 16-class problem with only ~200 traces creates severe class imbalance (some types have <10 examples), degrading the classifier's ability to learn robust boundaries; (3) the temporal window already implicitly captures type-sequence patterns that explicit type labels would provide. The slight improvement comes from the model learning that certain type transitions (e.g., Type-3 'reluctant compliance' → Type-4 'full compliance') are strong commitment indicators. Not enough to change any claims.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A3: Binary vs 16-Label Supervision\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Supervision\", \"Crossing Rate\", \"Lead Mean\", \"Acc\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"Binary\", \"0.767\", \"-1.1\", \"0.848\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"16-label\", \"0.791\", \"-0.3\", \"0.862\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"Binary\", \"0.67\", \"-1.8\", \"0.821\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"16-label\", \"0.70\", \"-1.2\", \"0.838\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A3: Confusion matrix for 16-label classifier on r1-8b test set, showing which label transitions the model captures vs misses.\"\n    analysis: \"Reports as P1 ablation addressing reviewer concern about underusing supervision. Conclusion: binary commitment label is near-sufficient for the detection task. Richer supervision could help with a larger dataset (>1000 traces) but current data cannot support 16-class temporal learning. Recommend as future work direction.\"\n\n  - name: \"Oracle Decomposition Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: \"Analyze why the label-oracle achieves +31 step lead time with 100% early detection while probes achieve +4.7 mean with 39% early detection. Characterize what label patterns encode that hidden states don't.\"\n    expected_results:\n      oracle_uses_future_context: true\n      oracle_lead_from_last_safety_label_gap: 31\n      probe_sees_current_state_only: true\n      traces_with_abrupt_commitment: 0.42\n      traces_with_gradual_decline: 0.58\n      gradual_traces_probe_lead_mean: 6.2\n      abrupt_traces_probe_lead_mean: -3.8\n      hidden_state_encodes_current_safety: true\n      hidden_state_encodes_future_trajectory: false\n      key_gap: \"oracle uses K-step lookahead (knows no future safety labels exist); probes must predict this from current state\"\n    result_rationale: \"The oracle's advantage is fundamentally about future knowledge — it defines commitment as 'last safety label + 1' which requires knowing the FUTURE contains no more safety labels. Probes can only observe current hidden states, which encode current safety reasoning state well (high per-step accuracy) but poorly predict whether safety reasoning will resume. The decomposition reveals two trace populations: (1) 'gradual decline' traces (58%) where safety reasoning frequency decreases over 15-30 steps before cessation — probes achieve +6.2 mean lead on these because the declining pattern is detectable; (2) 'abrupt commitment' traces (42%) where safety reasoning stops suddenly after a single decisive step — probes achieve -3.8 mean lead on these because there's no temporal pattern to anticipate. The GRU's overall +4.7 is a mixture of these two populations. This explains why median is 0: the abrupt traces pull the distribution left.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: Oracle Decomposition by Commitment Pattern\"\n        columns: [\"Pattern\", \"% Traces\", \"Oracle Lead\", \"GRU Lead Mean\", \"GRU Lead Median\", \"Temporal LR Crossing\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Gradual decline\", \"58%\", \"+38.2\", \"+6.2\", \"+3\", \"0.83\"]\n          - [\"Abrupt commitment\", \"42%\", \"+21.4\", \"-3.8\", \"-4\", \"0.61\"]\n          - [\"All\", \"100%\", \"+31.0\", \"+4.7\", \"0\", \"0.767\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 7: Dual-panel showing probe score timeseries for example gradual-decline trace (left, smooth transition, probe fires early) vs abrupt-commitment trace (right, sudden drop, probe fires late).\"\n    analysis: \"This is the key insight for the Discussion section: reliable pre-textual detection requires predicting whether safety reasoning will RESUME, not just whether it's currently absent. The gradual/abrupt decomposition explains all observed variance and motivates specific future directions: (1) multi-step predictive models that learn to forecast safety label sequences, (2) attention over longer contexts that might capture the precursors to abrupt commitment. BOCPD (exp-014) partially addresses this by detecting the distributional shift itself rather than predicting the future.\"\n\n  - name: \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B Base)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: \"4th model completing the 2×2 architecture×scale matrix (Llama-8B, Qwen-7B, Llama-based-32B proxy, Qwen-32B). Tests whether scale or architecture is the primary factor.\"\n    expected_results:\n      openthinker_7b_static_crossing: 0.44\n      openthinker_7b_temporal_w15_crossing: 0.63\n      openthinker_7b_temporal_delta: 0.19\n      openthinker_7b_gru_lead_mean: 2.1\n      openthinker_7b_gru_lead_median: 0\n      openthinker_7b_best_layer: 14\n      openthinker_7b_best_layer_depth: 0.44\n      scale_effect_qwen_7b_vs_32b_crossing_delta: -0.04\n      arch_effect_llama8b_vs_qwen7b_crossing_delta: -0.14\n    result_rationale: \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base) performs similarly to r1-8b on static probing (0.44 vs 0.465) but slightly worse on temporal (0.63 vs 0.767). The ~14pp temporal gap between Llama-8B and Qwen-7B at same scale reveals that architecture matters more than scale for temporal detection: Llama's attention patterns may create more temporally coherent safety-state representations. The 2×2 matrix shows: (1) scale effect is small (7B→32B within Qwen: 0.63→0.67, only +4pp); (2) architecture effect is larger (Qwen-7B vs Llama-8B: 0.63 vs 0.767, -14pp). This suggests Llama-based models are more amenable to temporal safety probing, possibly because Llama's pre-training creates more linearly separable temporal patterns. However, the temporal advantage over static is consistent across all 4 models (+19-30pp), confirming the core finding is robust.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A4: 2×2 Architecture × Scale Matrix (Temporal W=15 Crossing Rate)\"\n        columns: [\"\", \"~7-8B\", \"~32B\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Llama-based\", \"0.767 (r1-8b)\", \"— (no 32B Llama reasoning model)\"]\n          - [\"Qwen-based\", \"0.63 (OpenThinker-7B)\", \"0.67 (r1-32b) / 0.58 (QwQ)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A4: Grouped bar chart showing static vs temporal crossing rate for all 4 models, grouped by architecture family. Temporal advantage (bar height difference) is consistent but absolute levels vary.\"\n    analysis: \"Confirms that the temporal advantage is a genuine phenomenon across architectures and scales, not a Llama-specific artifact. The architecture > scale finding is interesting for the paper: it suggests that probing methods may need architecture-specific tuning. Reports in supplementary as P2 supporting evidence.\"\n\n  - name: \"GRU/Attention Probe on 32B Models\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: \"GRU-256 with 7-layer input on 32B models with full hyperparameter budget. Tests whether GRU's coverage advantage over LR persists at larger scale.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_gru_crossing: 0.65\n      r1_32b_gru_acc: 0.807\n      r1_32b_gru_vs_temporal_lr_delta: -0.02\n      qwq_32b_gru_crossing: 0.57\n      qwq_32b_gru_acc: 0.789\n      qwq_32b_gru_vs_temporal_lr_delta: -0.01\n      r1_32b_attention_crossing: 0.63\n      r1_32b_attention_acc: 0.798\n      overfitting_observed: true\n      early_stop_epoch_r1_32b: 8\n      early_stop_epoch_qwq: 6\n    result_rationale: \"On 32B models, GRU does NOT improve over temporal LR — in fact it performs comparably or slightly worse (-1 to -2pp). This occurs because: (1) with 5120-d × 7 layers = 35840-d input, the GRU's 256 hidden units are relatively undersized, creating an information bottleneck; (2) the same ~200 training traces are even more insufficient for the higher-dimensional GRU on 32B inputs, leading to earlier overfitting (epoch 6-8 vs epoch 14 on r1-8b); (3) the temporal LR with W=15 on 5120-d already has 76,800 parameters, making it a strong baseline that a small GRU cannot easily beat. This STRENGTHENS the 'simplicity is optimal' story — temporal LR is not only simpler but also more data-efficient. The attention probe performs slightly worse than GRU, confirming that at this data scale, additional architectural complexity hurts.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A5: GRU vs Temporal LR on 32B Models\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Temporal LR Crossing\", \"GRU Crossing\", \"Attention Crossing\", \"Delta (GRU-LR)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"0.767\", \"0.767*\", \"—\", \"+0.00\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"0.67\", \"0.65\", \"0.63\", \"-0.02\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"0.58\", \"0.57\", \"0.55\", \"-0.01\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A5: Training curves (val loss) for GRU on r1-8b vs r1-32b. r1-32b shows earlier divergence between train/val, confirming overfitting hypothesis.\"\n    analysis: \"Key narrative implication: on 32B models with current data sizes, temporal LR IS the best method. GRU's advantage on r1-8b reflects a favorable parameter-to-data ratio that doesn't hold at scale. This simplifies the paper's method story: recommend temporal LR as the default method, with GRU as an option when more training data is available. For Claim 3 (lead time), GRU on 32B achieves worse lead time than on r1-8b, further supporting the 'unreliable positive' characterization.\"\n\n  - name: \"BOCPD on Probe Score Sequences\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-014\"\n    description: \"Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (Adams & MacKay 2007) applied to per-step temporal LR probe confidence scores. Also test CUSUM as simpler alternative. Evaluates whether distributional shift detection achieves positive lead time.\"\n    expected_results:\n      bocpd_r1_8b_lead_mean: 5.8\n      bocpd_r1_8b_lead_median: 1\n      bocpd_r1_8b_positive_lead_rate: 0.48\n      bocpd_r1_8b_crossing_rate: 0.58\n      bocpd_r1_32b_lead_mean: 3.2\n      bocpd_r1_32b_lead_median: 0\n      bocpd_r1_32b_positive_lead_rate: 0.39\n      bocpd_r1_32b_crossing_rate: 0.49\n      bocpd_qwq_lead_mean: 1.9\n      bocpd_qwq_lead_median: 0\n      bocpd_qwq_positive_lead_rate: 0.35\n      bocpd_qwq_crossing_rate: 0.44\n      cusum_r1_8b_lead_mean: 4.1\n      cusum_r1_8b_lead_median: 0\n      cusum_r1_8b_positive_lead_rate: 0.42\n      cusum_r1_8b_crossing_rate: 0.53\n      bocpd_hazard_rate: 0.01\n      bocpd_observation_model: \"Gaussian with conjugate Normal-Gamma prior\"\n    result_rationale: \"BOCPD achieves positive mean lead time (5.8/3.2/1.9 across models) with marginally better positive-lead rate (48%/39%/35%) than GRU (39%/38%/31%). The median is 0-1, slightly better than GRU's 0. BOCPD succeeds where GRU partially fails because it's solving a different problem: detecting a distributional shift in the probe score sequence rather than predicting future states. When the probe score begins transitioning from 'safe' to 'committed,' BOCPD detects this transition in real-time, achieving 1-5 steps of lead before the probe score fully crosses the threshold. However, coverage drops relative to temporal LR (0.58 vs 0.767 on r1-8b) because BOCPD requires observing enough post-change data to confirm the change point — some traces commit too rapidly for the Bayesian posterior to accumulate evidence. The hazard rate (0.01) means BOCPD expects ~100 steps between change points, appropriate for these trace lengths. CUSUM performs slightly worse than BOCPD (no prior information exploitation). Cross-architecture: effect attenuates on 32B models because their probe score sequences are noisier (more false transitions), making change-point detection less reliable.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: BOCPD vs GRU Lead Time Comparison\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Method\", \"Lead Mean\", \"Lead Median\", \"Pos Lead Rate\", \"Crossing Rate\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"GRU (multi-layer)\", \"+4.7\", \"0\", \"39%\", \"0.767\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"BOCPD\", \"+5.8\", \"+1\", \"48%\", \"0.58\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"CUSUM\", \"+4.1\", \"0\", \"42%\", \"0.53\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"GRU\", \"+1.8\", \"0\", \"38%\", \"0.62\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"BOCPD\", \"+3.2\", \"0\", \"39%\", \"0.49\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"GRU\", \"+0.4\", \"-1\", \"31%\", \"0.55\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"BOCPD\", \"+1.9\", \"0\", \"35%\", \"0.44\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 8a: BOCPD run-length posterior visualization on an example trace, showing change-point probability spike near commitment point.\"\n      - \"Fig 8b: Lead time scatter (BOCPD vs GRU) per trace. Points above diagonal = BOCPD better. Roughly symmetric with slight BOCPD advantage in upper-left quadrant.\"\n      - \"Fig 8c: Precision-recall curve for BOCPD change-point detection across threshold values.\"\n    analysis: \"BOCPD provides a genuine (if marginal) improvement in lead time over GRU, with the added benefit of being a principled statistical method rather than a learned model. However, the improvement is inconsistent across architectures and comes at the cost of lower coverage. The coverage-vs-timing tradeoff is fundamental: BOCPD trades coverage for timing specificity. For the paper, BOCPD adds methodological novelty but doesn't resolve the 'unreliable positive' characterization of Claim 3. Frame as: 'BOCPD achieves the best positive lead rate (48% on r1-8b) among all methods tested, but still falls well short of the oracle (100%), confirming that reliable pre-textual detection remains an open problem.' The inconsistency across architectures (48%→35% positive lead rate) means we cannot claim BOCPD as a solved component.\"\n\n  - name: \"False Positive Rate Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-015\"\n    description: \"All probes (static LR, temporal LR, GRU, BOCPD) evaluated on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5). Reports FPR, precision-recall curves, and identifies usable operating points.\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_safe_traces: 87\n      static_lr_fpr: 0.18\n      temporal_lr_fpr: 0.22\n      gru_fpr: 0.15\n      bocpd_fpr: 0.12\n      temporal_lr_precision_at_80_recall: 0.71\n      temporal_lr_precision_at_60_recall: 0.82\n      gru_precision_at_80_recall: 0.74\n      gru_precision_at_60_recall: 0.86\n      bocpd_precision_at_80_recall: 0.68\n      bocpd_precision_at_60_recall: 0.79\n      safe_trace_probe_score_mean: 0.31\n      jailbreak_trace_probe_score_mean: 0.72\n      separation_gap: 0.41\n      threshold_for_10pct_fpr: 0.58\n      coverage_at_10pct_fpr: 0.52\n      threshold_for_20pct_fpr: 0.44\n      coverage_at_20pct_fpr: 0.71\n    result_rationale: \"FPR is moderate (12-22%) at default thresholds, reflecting the fact that safe traces also exhibit fluctuations in probe scores — safety reasoning naturally varies in density during benign problem-solving, and probes sometimes interpret low-safety-density periods as commitment-like. Temporal LR has the highest FPR (22%) because its longer context window amplifies transient low-safety periods into false alarms. GRU has lower FPR (15%) because its learned dynamics distinguish genuine commitment (permanent decline) from transient dips. BOCPD has lowest FPR (12%) because it requires sustained distributional shift, filtering out momentary fluctuations. The precision-recall curves show usable operating points exist: at 10% FPR, coverage drops to ~52% (acceptable for high-precision monitoring); at 20% FPR, coverage reaches ~71% (better for high-recall screening). The 0.41 separation gap between safe and jailbreak trace mean scores indicates the signal is real but distributions overlap, requiring threshold tuning per deployment context.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 9: False Positive Analysis Summary\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"FPR (default)\", \"Precision@80%Recall\", \"Precision@60%Recall\", \"AUROC\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Static LR\", \"0.18\", \"0.67\", \"0.78\", \"0.79\"]\n          - [\"Temporal LR W=15\", \"0.22\", \"0.71\", \"0.82\", \"0.82\"]\n          - [\"GRU (multi-layer)\", \"0.15\", \"0.74\", \"0.86\", \"0.85\"]\n          - [\"BOCPD\", \"0.12\", \"0.68\", \"0.79\", \"0.81\"]\n      - name: \"Table 9b: Operating Point Analysis (Temporal LR)\"\n        columns: [\"Target FPR\", \"Threshold\", \"Coverage\", \"Precision\", \"F1\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"5%\", \"0.65\", \"0.38\", \"0.92\", \"0.54\"]\n          - [\"10%\", \"0.58\", \"0.52\", \"0.85\", \"0.64\"]\n          - [\"15%\", \"0.51\", \"0.63\", \"0.79\", \"0.70\"]\n          - [\"20%\", \"0.44\", \"0.71\", \"0.73\", \"0.72\"]\n          - [\"25%\", \"0.38\", \"0.76\", \"0.68\", \"0.72\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 9a: Precision-recall curves for all methods. GRU dominates in the high-precision region; temporal LR dominates in the high-recall region.\"\n      - \"Fig 9b: Probe score distribution (kernel density) for safe vs jailbreak traces. Overlap region highlighted, showing why FPR is non-trivial.\"\n      - \"Fig 9c: ROC curves with operating points annotated.\"\n    analysis: \"The FPR analysis reveals that deployment requires explicit threshold tuning — no method achieves both high coverage and low FPR at a single operating point. The best tradeoff depends on deployment context: high-security environments (prefer low FPR) should use BOCPD or high-threshold temporal LR; broad monitoring (prefer high coverage) can tolerate 20% FPR. For the paper, this adds a critical section that addresses reviewer concern about deployment utility. The key message: 'Usable operating points exist (e.g., 85% precision at 52% coverage, or 73% precision at 71% coverage), but require threshold calibration on a per-deployment basis.' This is honest about limitations while showing the method has practical value.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12133,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12133},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ec1257e8-db47-4220-9b31-3f50b7783d8d","uuid":"a494b86c-9867-4607-b5dc-80c970000a53"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":272110,"duration_api_ms":273269,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: \"Claim 1 holds but needs qualifier ('on most architectures tested'). Claim 2 softens from 'saturates oracle' to 'substantially improves over static' — coverage advantage is real but model-dependent (+15-30pp range). Claim 3 unchanged (GRU/BOCPD both achieve positive mean but unreliable). Cross-architecture story becomes 'phenomenon is consistent but effect magnitudes vary.' Drop 'cross-model transfer' as a claim, report as ablation. FPR discussion added: precision-recall curves show usable operating points exist but require threshold tuning. Reframe contribution as 'first systematic evaluation revealing what does/doesn't transfer.' Submit EMNLP Main but prepare for Findings redirect.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Cross-Architecture Static Probe (32B Models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: \"Layer sweep + static LogisticRegression probe on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B (64 layers, 5120-d hidden states). Identifies best layer, evaluates static detection coverage.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_best_layer_idx: 29\n      r1_32b_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.453\n      r1_32b_best_acc: 0.793\n      r1_32b_best_f1: 0.612\n      r1_32b_static_crossing_rate: 0.42\n      r1_32b_static_lead_mean: -3.1\n      r1_32b_fisher_orig: 14.8\n      r1_32b_fisher_pca3: 0.87\n      r1_32b_var_explained_3pc: 0.142\n      qwq_32b_best_layer_idx: 31\n      qwq_32b_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.484\n      qwq_32b_best_acc: 0.781\n      qwq_32b_best_f1: 0.589\n      qwq_32b_static_crossing_rate: 0.38\n      qwq_32b_static_lead_mean: -4.2\n      qwq_32b_fisher_orig: 13.2\n      qwq_32b_fisher_pca3: 0.81\n      qwq_32b_var_explained_3pc: 0.131\n    result_rationale: \"32B models show the same qualitative pattern as r1-8b — high Fisher discriminant in full space but collapse under PCA — confirming that safety signal exists but is distributed across minor principal components. However, the 5120-d space is sparser relative to training data (same ~209 traces, higher dimensionality), so static LR accuracy drops ~2-4pp. Crossing rates land at 0.38-0.42, above the trivial floor but well below temporal methods, because static probes still cannot capture the temporal density-decline signature. Best layers cluster around 45% depth (layers 29-31), consistent with r1-8b finding that mid-layers encode safety state better than final layers which are optimized for next-token prediction. QwQ-32B performs slightly worse than r1-32b because it uses a different reasoning training recipe (reinforcement learning vs distillation), potentially distributing safety reasoning signal differently across layers.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Cross-Architecture Layer Sweep Summary\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Best Layer\", \"Depth %\", \"Acc\", \"F1\", \"Crossing Rate\", \"Lead Mean\", \"Fisher (orig)\", \"Fisher (PCA-3)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"14\", \"43.8%\", \"0.817\", \"0.646\", \"0.465\", \"-2.0\", \"17.9\", \"0.93\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (Qwen)\", \"29\", \"45.3%\", \"0.793\", \"0.612\", \"0.42\", \"-3.1\", \"14.8\", \"0.87\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\", \"31\", \"48.4%\", \"0.781\", \"0.589\", \"0.38\", \"-4.2\", \"13.2\", \"0.81\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3a: Layer sweep accuracy heatmap (64 layers × 2 models). Clear peak in layers 27-33 for both 32B models, broader plateau than r1-8b's sharp peak at layer 14.\"\n      - \"Fig 3b: Fisher discriminant ratio per layer (original vs PCA-3) for all 3 models. All show 15-20× collapse from full-space to PCA-3, confirming minor-component signal distribution is architecture-independent.\"\n    analysis: \"Static probes on 32B confirm Claim 1 generalizes: detection coverage remains below 50% regardless of architecture. The ~5pp drop in crossing rate (0.465→0.38-0.42) reflects the curse of dimensionality — 5120-d hidden states with only ~200 training traces create a harder linear separation problem. Fisher ratios are slightly lower (13-15 vs 17-18) suggesting safety signal is somewhat more diffuse in larger models, possibly because 32B models have more capacity to entangle safety reasoning with other computations. Key finding: best-layer depth percentage is remarkably consistent (44-48%) across 32-layer and 64-layer architectures, suggesting a universal 'safety encoding depth' in reasoning models.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cross-Architecture Temporal Window Probe (32B Models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: \"Temporal LR with W=15 on r1-32b and QwQ-32B at their respective best layers. Validates whether +30pp coverage improvement from temporal context holds cross-architecture.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.821\n      r1_32b_temporal_w15_crossing_rate: 0.67\n      r1_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean: -1.8\n      r1_32b_temporal_delta_over_static: 0.25\n      qwq_32b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.804\n      qwq_32b_temporal_w15_crossing_rate: 0.58\n      qwq_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean: -3.2\n      qwq_32b_temporal_delta_over_static: 0.20\n      cross_arch_mean_temporal_crossing: 0.625\n      cross_arch_mean_temporal_lead: -2.5\n    result_rationale: \"Temporal context improves coverage on 32B models (+20-25pp over static), but the gain is weaker than r1-8b's +30pp. This attenuation occurs because: (1) 32B models have longer reasoning traces (median ~80 steps vs ~55 for r1-8b), so the W=15 window captures a smaller fraction of the relevant temporal context; (2) safety reasoning density decline patterns are noisier in 32B models — these models interleave safety and task reasoning more fluidly, so the 'clean transition' that W=15 captures on r1-8b is spread across a wider window. r1-32b outperforms QwQ-32B because as a distilled model it more closely mirrors the teacher's reasoning structure, while QwQ's RL-trained reasoning is more variable. The coverage improvement is real (+20-25pp) but model-dependent, requiring Claim 2 to be qualified with 'on most architectures tested, with effect size varying by model.'\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Temporal Window LR (W=15) Cross-Architecture\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Static Crossing\", \"Temporal Crossing\", \"Delta\", \"Temporal Lead Mean\", \"Temporal Acc\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"0.465\", \"0.767\", \"+0.302\", \"-1.1\", \"0.848\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (Qwen)\", \"0.42\", \"0.67\", \"+0.25\", \"-1.8\", \"0.821\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\", \"0.38\", \"0.58\", \"+0.20\", \"-3.2\", \"0.804\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 4a: Bar chart showing static vs temporal crossing rate per model. All models show improvement; error bars (bootstrap 95% CI) overlap between r1-32b and QwQ but both are clearly above their static baselines.\"\n      - \"Fig 4b: Detection delay CDF per model. r1-8b CDF rises fastest (best detection timing), r1-32b intermediate, QwQ-32B has heaviest right tail.\"\n    analysis: \"The temporal advantage is robust (consistently +20-30pp) but not uniform. The oracle ceiling analysis suggests r1-8b's 76.7% may already be near-oracle for that model, while 32B models have higher oracle ceilings (longer traces = more opportunity) that temporal LR doesn't fully exploit. This motivates the window size ablation (exp-007) — W=15 may be suboptimal for 32B models with longer traces. For the paper narrative, Claim 2 becomes: 'Temporal context substantially improves detection coverage across architectures (+20-30pp), though the magnitude is model-dependent and may not reach oracle ceiling on all models.'\"\n\n  - name: \"Cross-Model Transfer\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: \"Zero-shot transfer: train on r1-8b (Llama, 4096-d), test on 32B models (Qwen, 5120-d) via learned projection head. Bidirectional: also train on Qwen models, test on Llama.\"\n    expected_results:\n      llama_to_qwen_r1_32b_crossing_retention: 0.48\n      llama_to_qwen_r1_32b_acc: 0.692\n      llama_to_qwen_qwq_crossing_retention: 0.41\n      llama_to_qwen_qwq_acc: 0.671\n      qwen_to_llama_crossing_retention: 0.52\n      qwen_to_llama_acc: 0.714\n      mean_cross_transfer_retention: 0.47\n      projection_head_dim: \"4096→5120 linear + ReLU + 5120\"\n      within_family_transfer_qwen32_to_qwq: 0.78\n      within_family_transfer_r1_8b_self_cv: 0.95\n    result_rationale: \"Cross-architecture transfer retains only ~40-50% of within-model coverage. The degradation is expected because: (1) hidden-state representations are not aligned across architectures — even same-family models (Qwen-based r1-32b vs QwQ) have different training objectives that shape representation geometry differently; (2) the linear projection head (4096→5120) cannot learn the nonlinear manifold alignment needed for full transfer; (3) safety signal is encoded in architecture-specific minor principal components — the specific directions that carry safety information differ across models. Within-family transfer (Qwen→Qwen) retains ~78%, confirming that shared pre-training creates partially aligned safety representations. The asymmetry (Qwen→Llama slightly better than Llama→Qwen) likely reflects that 32B models have richer representations that project down more gracefully than 8B representations project up. This result means cross-model transfer should be reported as an ablation, not a claim.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Cross-Model Transfer Matrix (Temporal LR W=15, Crossing Rate)\"\n        columns: [\"Train →\", \"Test: r1-8b\", \"Test: r1-32b\", \"Test: QwQ-32B\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"0.767 (self)\", \"0.32\", \"0.27\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (Qwen)\", \"0.35\", \"0.67 (self)\", \"0.52\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\", \"0.31\", \"0.48\", \"0.58 (self)\"]\n      - name: \"Table 5b: Transfer Retention Rate\"\n        columns: [\"Direction\", \"Retention %\", \"Acc Drop\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Llama→Qwen (r1-32b)\", \"48%\", \"-12.9pp\"]\n          - [\"Llama→Qwen (QwQ)\", \"41%\", \"-14.6pp\"]\n          - [\"Qwen→Llama\", \"52%\", \"-10.3pp\"]\n          - [\"Within-Qwen (r1-32b→QwQ)\", \"78%\", \"-4.1pp\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 5: Transfer matrix heatmap. Diagonal (within-model) is brightest. Within-family (Qwen-Qwen) is intermediate. Cross-family cells are darkest, showing 50%+ degradation.\"\n    analysis: \"Cross-model transfer is too unreliable (<50% retention cross-family) to present as a paper claim. However, within-family retention of 78% suggests that pre-training alignment partially preserves safety signal geometry. For the paper, this becomes an ablation in Section 5 with the takeaway: 'Safety commitment representations are model-specific; zero-shot transfer requires architecture-aware alignment that simple projection cannot provide.' This motivates future work on universal safety probes but does not undermine the per-model results.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cross-Architecture Lead Time Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: \"GRU sequence model (256-d, 7-layer input) on 32B models. Report lead time distribution and compare to r1-8b's +4.7 mean.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_mean: 1.8\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_median: 0\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_std: 11.2\n      r1_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.38\n      r1_32b_gru_crossing_rate: 0.62\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean: 0.4\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_median: -1\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_std: 14.7\n      qwq_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.31\n      qwq_32b_gru_crossing_rate: 0.55\n      cross_arch_mean_lead: 1.1\n      cross_arch_positive_lead_rate: 0.345\n    result_rationale: \"GRU lead time degrades substantially on 32B models (+1.8 and +0.4 vs +4.7 on r1-8b). This happens because: (1) 32B models have longer, more complex reasoning traces where the commitment point is less temporally predictable — the GRU must learn longer-range dependencies; (2) with the same ~200 training traces but higher-dimensional input (5120-d × 7 layers = 35840-d concatenated), the GRU is severely underfitting relative to the signal complexity; (3) QwQ-32B's RL-trained reasoning produces more variable commitment dynamics (sometimes abrupt, sometimes gradual), making sequential prediction harder. The median remains 0 or negative, confirming that pre-textual detection is unreliable across architectures — this is not an r1-8b artifact but a fundamental difficulty. Claim 3 narrative holds: positive mean lead exists but is architecture-dependent and unreliable.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: GRU Lead Time Distribution Across Architectures\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Lead Mean\", \"Lead Median\", \"Lead Std\", \"Pos Lead Rate\", \"Crossing Rate\", \"N Detected\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"+4.7\", \"0\", \"13.0\", \"39% (13/33)\", \"0.767\", \"33\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (Qwen)\", \"+1.8\", \"0\", \"11.2\", \"38% (11/29)\", \"0.62\", \"29\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\", \"+0.4\", \"-1\", \"14.7\", \"31% (8/26)\", \"0.55\", \"26\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 6a: Lead time distribution (violin plot) per model. All three show wide spread centered near 0, with r1-8b having the longest right tail.\"\n      - \"Fig 6b: CDF of lead time. Oracle (dashed) achieves 100% positive lead. All GRU curves cross 50% near 0, showing median-zero pattern is consistent.\"\n    analysis: \"The cross-architecture lead time results strengthen the 'fundamental limitation' narrative of Claim 3. Mean lead time is positive on all models but the effect attenuates with model scale (4.7→1.8→0.4), suggesting larger models encode commitment decisions in ways that are harder to anticipate from hidden-state sequences. This is consistent with the hypothesis that 32B models have more 'internal deliberation' before commitment — the hidden states don't leak the decision as early. For the paper, we report this as: 'Pre-textual detection signal exists across architectures (positive mean lead) but is weak and model-dependent, with no model achieving reliable (>50%) early detection.'\"\n\n  - name: \"Window Size Ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: \"Temporal LR with varying window sizes on all 3 models. Finds optimal W and characterizes diminishing returns.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_w1_crossing: 0.465\n      r1_8b_w3_crossing: 0.535\n      r1_8b_w5_crossing: 0.651\n      r1_8b_w10_crossing: 0.721\n      r1_8b_w15_crossing: 0.767\n      r1_8b_w20_crossing: 0.767\n      r1_8b_w25_crossing: 0.744\n      r1_32b_w1_crossing: 0.42\n      r1_32b_w5_crossing: 0.51\n      r1_32b_w10_crossing: 0.60\n      r1_32b_w15_crossing: 0.67\n      r1_32b_w20_crossing: 0.70\n      r1_32b_w25_crossing: 0.69\n      qwq_32b_w1_crossing: 0.38\n      qwq_32b_w5_crossing: 0.46\n      qwq_32b_w10_crossing: 0.54\n      qwq_32b_w15_crossing: 0.58\n      qwq_32b_w20_crossing: 0.61\n      qwq_32b_w25_crossing: 0.60\n      optimal_w_r1_8b: 15\n      optimal_w_r1_32b: 20\n      optimal_w_qwq: 20\n    result_rationale: \"Diminishing returns consistently appear around W=15-20, but 32B models benefit from slightly larger windows (optimal W=20 vs W=15 for r1-8b). This makes physical sense: 32B models have longer reasoning traces (~80 steps median vs ~55), so the 'safety reasoning density decline' pattern that temporal LR captures spans more steps. W=25 shows slight degradation due to overfitting (more parameters with same training data) and dilution of the relevant temporal signal with distant-past information. The W=1 baseline equals static performance (as expected — it's mathematically identical), confirming the improvement is genuinely from temporal context, not hyperparameter differences. The steepest improvement is W=1→W=5 on all models, suggesting the critical signal is in the recent 5-step history (current safety state), with W=5→W=15 adding the trend information.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A1: Window Size × Model Crossing Rate\"\n        columns: [\"W\", \"r1-8b\", \"r1-32b\", \"QwQ-32B\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"1\", \"0.465\", \"0.42\", \"0.38\"]\n          - [\"3\", \"0.535\", \"0.47\", \"0.42\"]\n          - [\"5\", \"0.651\", \"0.51\", \"0.46\"]\n          - [\"10\", \"0.721\", \"0.60\", \"0.54\"]\n          - [\"15\", \"0.767\", \"0.67\", \"0.58\"]\n          - [\"20\", \"0.767\", \"0.70\", \"0.61\"]\n          - [\"25\", \"0.744\", \"0.69\", \"0.60\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A1: Line plot of crossing rate vs window size, one curve per model. All show monotonic increase saturating around W=15-20, with slight decline at W=25. r1-8b curve is highest throughout.\"\n    analysis: \"Supports the paper's claim that temporal context is the key ingredient, not model complexity. The saturation at W=15-20 is interpretable: commitment points follow a period of declining safety reasoning that unfolds over roughly 10-20 reasoning steps. This connects to the oracle's +31-step mean lead — the relevant history window is shorter than the oracle's full lookahead, explaining why temporal LR captures coverage but not lead time.\"\n\n  - name: \"Layer Selection Ablation for Temporal Probe (32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: \"Temporal LR W=15 evaluated at all 64 layers (or representative subset) for both 32B models. Confirms middle-layer finding generalizes.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_best_temporal_layer: 30\n      r1_32b_layer_range_within_1pp: \"26-35\"\n      r1_32b_early_layers_avg_crossing: 0.31\n      r1_32b_mid_layers_avg_crossing: 0.64\n      r1_32b_late_layers_avg_crossing: 0.48\n      qwq_32b_best_temporal_layer: 33\n      qwq_32b_layer_range_within_1pp: \"28-37\"\n      qwq_32b_early_layers_avg_crossing: 0.28\n      qwq_32b_mid_layers_avg_crossing: 0.56\n      qwq_32b_late_layers_avg_crossing: 0.42\n    result_rationale: \"Middle layers (40-55% depth) dominate for temporal probing on 32B models, extending the r1-8b finding (layer 14/32 = 44%). This is consistent with the representation engineering literature — middle layers encode abstract semantic features while later layers are increasingly specialized for output distribution. The 'plateau' (layers within 1pp of best) is broader on 64-layer models (~10 layers vs ~5 for r1-8b's 32 layers), suggesting redundancy in safety encoding at larger scales. Late layers perform worse because they've begun specializing for next-token prediction, compressing the abstract safety-state signal. Early layers perform worst because safety reasoning hasn't yet been processed into a separable representation.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A2: Layer Depth vs Temporal Probe Performance\"\n        columns: [\"Depth Range\", \"r1-32b Crossing\", \"QwQ Crossing\", \"r1-8b (reference)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"0-25% (layers 1-16)\", \"0.31\", \"0.28\", \"0.34\"]\n          - [\"25-50% (layers 17-32)\", \"0.64\", \"0.53\", \"0.72*\"]\n          - [\"50-75% (layers 33-48)\", \"0.57\", \"0.56\", \"0.58\"]\n          - [\"75-100% (layers 49-64)\", \"0.48\", \"0.42\", \"0.41\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A2: Layer index vs crossing rate, color-coded by model. Clear inverted-U shape with peak at 40-55% depth on all models.\"\n    analysis: \"The middle-layer advantage generalizes robustly. For the paper, this means layer selection is not a critical hyperparameter — any layer in the 40-55% depth range performs near-optimally. This is practically important: practitioners don't need expensive layer sweeps, they can use depth-percentage heuristic. Included as supplementary material.\"\n\n  - name: \"16-Label Taxonomy Supervision\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: \"Train temporal LR using fine-grained safety labels (4 types × safe/unsafe × reasoning/action) instead of binary commitment label. Test if richer supervision improves coverage or timing.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_16label_crossing_rate: 0.791\n      r1_8b_16label_lead_mean: -0.3\n      r1_8b_16label_acc: 0.862\n      r1_8b_binary_crossing_rate_ref: 0.767\n      r1_8b_16label_improvement_crossing: 0.024\n      r1_8b_16label_improvement_lead: 0.8\n      r1_32b_16label_crossing_rate: 0.70\n      r1_32b_16label_lead_mean: -1.2\n    result_rationale: \"16-label supervision provides marginal improvement (+2-3pp crossing rate, ~+1 step lead time) over binary. The gain is small because: (1) the binary commitment label already captures the critical transition (safety reasoning ceases), and finer-grained types add noise rather than signal for this specific detection task; (2) the 16-class problem with only ~200 traces creates severe class imbalance (some types have <10 examples), degrading the classifier's ability to learn robust boundaries; (3) the temporal window already implicitly captures type-sequence patterns that explicit type labels would provide. The slight improvement comes from the model learning that certain type transitions (e.g., Type-3 'reluctant compliance' → Type-4 'full compliance') are strong commitment indicators. Not enough to change any claims.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A3: Binary vs 16-Label Supervision\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Supervision\", \"Crossing Rate\", \"Lead Mean\", \"Acc\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"Binary\", \"0.767\", \"-1.1\", \"0.848\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"16-label\", \"0.791\", \"-0.3\", \"0.862\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"Binary\", \"0.67\", \"-1.8\", \"0.821\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"16-label\", \"0.70\", \"-1.2\", \"0.838\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A3: Confusion matrix for 16-label classifier on r1-8b test set, showing which label transitions the model captures vs misses.\"\n    analysis: \"Reports as P1 ablation addressing reviewer concern about underusing supervision. Conclusion: binary commitment label is near-sufficient for the detection task. Richer supervision could help with a larger dataset (>1000 traces) but current data cannot support 16-class temporal learning. Recommend as future work direction.\"\n\n  - name: \"Oracle Decomposition Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: \"Analyze why the label-oracle achieves +31 step lead time with 100% early detection while probes achieve +4.7 mean with 39% early detection. Characterize what label patterns encode that hidden states don't.\"\n    expected_results:\n      oracle_uses_future_context: true\n      oracle_lead_from_last_safety_label_gap: 31\n      probe_sees_current_state_only: true\n      traces_with_abrupt_commitment: 0.42\n      traces_with_gradual_decline: 0.58\n      gradual_traces_probe_lead_mean: 6.2\n      abrupt_traces_probe_lead_mean: -3.8\n      hidden_state_encodes_current_safety: true\n      hidden_state_encodes_future_trajectory: false\n      key_gap: \"oracle uses K-step lookahead (knows no future safety labels exist); probes must predict this from current state\"\n    result_rationale: \"The oracle's advantage is fundamentally about future knowledge — it defines commitment as 'last safety label + 1' which requires knowing the FUTURE contains no more safety labels. Probes can only observe current hidden states, which encode current safety reasoning state well (high per-step accuracy) but poorly predict whether safety reasoning will resume. The decomposition reveals two trace populations: (1) 'gradual decline' traces (58%) where safety reasoning frequency decreases over 15-30 steps before cessation — probes achieve +6.2 mean lead on these because the declining pattern is detectable; (2) 'abrupt commitment' traces (42%) where safety reasoning stops suddenly after a single decisive step — probes achieve -3.8 mean lead on these because there's no temporal pattern to anticipate. The GRU's overall +4.7 is a mixture of these two populations. This explains why median is 0: the abrupt traces pull the distribution left.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: Oracle Decomposition by Commitment Pattern\"\n        columns: [\"Pattern\", \"% Traces\", \"Oracle Lead\", \"GRU Lead Mean\", \"GRU Lead Median\", \"Temporal LR Crossing\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Gradual decline\", \"58%\", \"+38.2\", \"+6.2\", \"+3\", \"0.83\"]\n          - [\"Abrupt commitment\", \"42%\", \"+21.4\", \"-3.8\", \"-4\", \"0.61\"]\n          - [\"All\", \"100%\", \"+31.0\", \"+4.7\", \"0\", \"0.767\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 7: Dual-panel showing probe score timeseries for example gradual-decline trace (left, smooth transition, probe fires early) vs abrupt-commitment trace (right, sudden drop, probe fires late).\"\n    analysis: \"This is the key insight for the Discussion section: reliable pre-textual detection requires predicting whether safety reasoning will RESUME, not just whether it's currently absent. The gradual/abrupt decomposition explains all observed variance and motivates specific future directions: (1) multi-step predictive models that learn to forecast safety label sequences, (2) attention over longer contexts that might capture the precursors to abrupt commitment. BOCPD (exp-014) partially addresses this by detecting the distributional shift itself rather than predicting the future.\"\n\n  - name: \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B Base)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: \"4th model completing the 2×2 architecture×scale matrix (Llama-8B, Qwen-7B, Llama-based-32B proxy, Qwen-32B). Tests whether scale or architecture is the primary factor.\"\n    expected_results:\n      openthinker_7b_static_crossing: 0.44\n      openthinker_7b_temporal_w15_crossing: 0.63\n      openthinker_7b_temporal_delta: 0.19\n      openthinker_7b_gru_lead_mean: 2.1\n      openthinker_7b_gru_lead_median: 0\n      openthinker_7b_best_layer: 14\n      openthinker_7b_best_layer_depth: 0.44\n      scale_effect_qwen_7b_vs_32b_crossing_delta: -0.04\n      arch_effect_llama8b_vs_qwen7b_crossing_delta: -0.14\n    result_rationale: \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base) performs similarly to r1-8b on static probing (0.44 vs 0.465) but slightly worse on temporal (0.63 vs 0.767). The ~14pp temporal gap between Llama-8B and Qwen-7B at same scale reveals that architecture matters more than scale for temporal detection: Llama's attention patterns may create more temporally coherent safety-state representations. The 2×2 matrix shows: (1) scale effect is small (7B→32B within Qwen: 0.63→0.67, only +4pp); (2) architecture effect is larger (Qwen-7B vs Llama-8B: 0.63 vs 0.767, -14pp). This suggests Llama-based models are more amenable to temporal safety probing, possibly because Llama's pre-training creates more linearly separable temporal patterns. However, the temporal advantage over static is consistent across all 4 models (+19-30pp), confirming the core finding is robust.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A4: 2×2 Architecture × Scale Matrix (Temporal W=15 Crossing Rate)\"\n        columns: [\"\", \"~7-8B\", \"~32B\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Llama-based\", \"0.767 (r1-8b)\", \"— (no 32B Llama reasoning model)\"]\n          - [\"Qwen-based\", \"0.63 (OpenThinker-7B)\", \"0.67 (r1-32b) / 0.58 (QwQ)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A4: Grouped bar chart showing static vs temporal crossing rate for all 4 models, grouped by architecture family. Temporal advantage (bar height difference) is consistent but absolute levels vary.\"\n    analysis: \"Confirms that the temporal advantage is a genuine phenomenon across architectures and scales, not a Llama-specific artifact. The architecture > scale finding is interesting for the paper: it suggests that probing methods may need architecture-specific tuning. Reports in supplementary as P2 supporting evidence.\"\n\n  - name: \"GRU/Attention Probe on 32B Models\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: \"GRU-256 with 7-layer input on 32B models with full hyperparameter budget. Tests whether GRU's coverage advantage over LR persists at larger scale.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_gru_crossing: 0.65\n      r1_32b_gru_acc: 0.807\n      r1_32b_gru_vs_temporal_lr_delta: -0.02\n      qwq_32b_gru_crossing: 0.57\n      qwq_32b_gru_acc: 0.789\n      qwq_32b_gru_vs_temporal_lr_delta: -0.01\n      r1_32b_attention_crossing: 0.63\n      r1_32b_attention_acc: 0.798\n      overfitting_observed: true\n      early_stop_epoch_r1_32b: 8\n      early_stop_epoch_qwq: 6\n    result_rationale: \"On 32B models, GRU does NOT improve over temporal LR — in fact it performs comparably or slightly worse (-1 to -2pp). This occurs because: (1) with 5120-d × 7 layers = 35840-d input, the GRU's 256 hidden units are relatively undersized, creating an information bottleneck; (2) the same ~200 training traces are even more insufficient for the higher-dimensional GRU on 32B inputs, leading to earlier overfitting (epoch 6-8 vs epoch 14 on r1-8b); (3) the temporal LR with W=15 on 5120-d already has 76,800 parameters, making it a strong baseline that a small GRU cannot easily beat. This STRENGTHENS the 'simplicity is optimal' story — temporal LR is not only simpler but also more data-efficient. The attention probe performs slightly worse than GRU, confirming that at this data scale, additional architectural complexity hurts.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A5: GRU vs Temporal LR on 32B Models\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Temporal LR Crossing\", \"GRU Crossing\", \"Attention Crossing\", \"Delta (GRU-LR)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"0.767\", \"0.767*\", \"—\", \"+0.00\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"0.67\", \"0.65\", \"0.63\", \"-0.02\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"0.58\", \"0.57\", \"0.55\", \"-0.01\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A5: Training curves (val loss) for GRU on r1-8b vs r1-32b. r1-32b shows earlier divergence between train/val, confirming overfitting hypothesis.\"\n    analysis: \"Key narrative implication: on 32B models with current data sizes, temporal LR IS the best method. GRU's advantage on r1-8b reflects a favorable parameter-to-data ratio that doesn't hold at scale. This simplifies the paper's method story: recommend temporal LR as the default method, with GRU as an option when more training data is available. For Claim 3 (lead time), GRU on 32B achieves worse lead time than on r1-8b, further supporting the 'unreliable positive' characterization.\"\n\n  - name: \"BOCPD on Probe Score Sequences\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-014\"\n    description: \"Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (Adams & MacKay 2007) applied to per-step temporal LR probe confidence scores. Also test CUSUM as simpler alternative. Evaluates whether distributional shift detection achieves positive lead time.\"\n    expected_results:\n      bocpd_r1_8b_lead_mean: 5.8\n      bocpd_r1_8b_lead_median: 1\n      bocpd_r1_8b_positive_lead_rate: 0.48\n      bocpd_r1_8b_crossing_rate: 0.58\n      bocpd_r1_32b_lead_mean: 3.2\n      bocpd_r1_32b_lead_median: 0\n      bocpd_r1_32b_positive_lead_rate: 0.39\n      bocpd_r1_32b_crossing_rate: 0.49\n      bocpd_qwq_lead_mean: 1.9\n      bocpd_qwq_lead_median: 0\n      bocpd_qwq_positive_lead_rate: 0.35\n      bocpd_qwq_crossing_rate: 0.44\n      cusum_r1_8b_lead_mean: 4.1\n      cusum_r1_8b_lead_median: 0\n      cusum_r1_8b_positive_lead_rate: 0.42\n      cusum_r1_8b_crossing_rate: 0.53\n      bocpd_hazard_rate: 0.01\n      bocpd_observation_model: \"Gaussian with conjugate Normal-Gamma prior\"\n    result_rationale: \"BOCPD achieves positive mean lead time (5.8/3.2/1.9 across models) with marginally better positive-lead rate (48%/39%/35%) than GRU (39%/38%/31%). The median is 0-1, slightly better than GRU's 0. BOCPD succeeds where GRU partially fails because it's solving a different problem: detecting a distributional shift in the probe score sequence rather than predicting future states. When the probe score begins transitioning from 'safe' to 'committed,' BOCPD detects this transition in real-time, achieving 1-5 steps of lead before the probe score fully crosses the threshold. However, coverage drops relative to temporal LR (0.58 vs 0.767 on r1-8b) because BOCPD requires observing enough post-change data to confirm the change point — some traces commit too rapidly for the Bayesian posterior to accumulate evidence. The hazard rate (0.01) means BOCPD expects ~100 steps between change points, appropriate for these trace lengths. CUSUM performs slightly worse than BOCPD (no prior information exploitation). Cross-architecture: effect attenuates on 32B models because their probe score sequences are noisier (more false transitions), making change-point detection less reliable.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: BOCPD vs GRU Lead Time Comparison\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Method\", \"Lead Mean\", \"Lead Median\", \"Pos Lead Rate\", \"Crossing Rate\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"GRU (multi-layer)\", \"+4.7\", \"0\", \"39%\", \"0.767\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"BOCPD\", \"+5.8\", \"+1\", \"48%\", \"0.58\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"CUSUM\", \"+4.1\", \"0\", \"42%\", \"0.53\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"GRU\", \"+1.8\", \"0\", \"38%\", \"0.62\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"BOCPD\", \"+3.2\", \"0\", \"39%\", \"0.49\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"GRU\", \"+0.4\", \"-1\", \"31%\", \"0.55\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"BOCPD\", \"+1.9\", \"0\", \"35%\", \"0.44\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 8a: BOCPD run-length posterior visualization on an example trace, showing change-point probability spike near commitment point.\"\n      - \"Fig 8b: Lead time scatter (BOCPD vs GRU) per trace. Points above diagonal = BOCPD better. Roughly symmetric with slight BOCPD advantage in upper-left quadrant.\"\n      - \"Fig 8c: Precision-recall curve for BOCPD change-point detection across threshold values.\"\n    analysis: \"BOCPD provides a genuine (if marginal) improvement in lead time over GRU, with the added benefit of being a principled statistical method rather than a learned model. However, the improvement is inconsistent across architectures and comes at the cost of lower coverage. The coverage-vs-timing tradeoff is fundamental: BOCPD trades coverage for timing specificity. For the paper, BOCPD adds methodological novelty but doesn't resolve the 'unreliable positive' characterization of Claim 3. Frame as: 'BOCPD achieves the best positive lead rate (48% on r1-8b) among all methods tested, but still falls well short of the oracle (100%), confirming that reliable pre-textual detection remains an open problem.' The inconsistency across architectures (48%→35% positive lead rate) means we cannot claim BOCPD as a solved component.\"\n\n  - name: \"False Positive Rate Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-015\"\n    description: \"All probes (static LR, temporal LR, GRU, BOCPD) evaluated on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5). Reports FPR, precision-recall curves, and identifies usable operating points.\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_safe_traces: 87\n      static_lr_fpr: 0.18\n      temporal_lr_fpr: 0.22\n      gru_fpr: 0.15\n      bocpd_fpr: 0.12\n      temporal_lr_precision_at_80_recall: 0.71\n      temporal_lr_precision_at_60_recall: 0.82\n      gru_precision_at_80_recall: 0.74\n      gru_precision_at_60_recall: 0.86\n      bocpd_precision_at_80_recall: 0.68\n      bocpd_precision_at_60_recall: 0.79\n      safe_trace_probe_score_mean: 0.31\n      jailbreak_trace_probe_score_mean: 0.72\n      separation_gap: 0.41\n      threshold_for_10pct_fpr: 0.58\n      coverage_at_10pct_fpr: 0.52\n      threshold_for_20pct_fpr: 0.44\n      coverage_at_20pct_fpr: 0.71\n    result_rationale: \"FPR is moderate (12-22%) at default thresholds, reflecting the fact that safe traces also exhibit fluctuations in probe scores — safety reasoning naturally varies in density during benign problem-solving, and probes sometimes interpret low-safety-density periods as commitment-like. Temporal LR has the highest FPR (22%) because its longer context window amplifies transient low-safety periods into false alarms. GRU has lower FPR (15%) because its learned dynamics distinguish genuine commitment (permanent decline) from transient dips. BOCPD has lowest FPR (12%) because it requires sustained distributional shift, filtering out momentary fluctuations. The precision-recall curves show usable operating points exist: at 10% FPR, coverage drops to ~52% (acceptable for high-precision monitoring); at 20% FPR, coverage reaches ~71% (better for high-recall screening). The 0.41 separation gap between safe and jailbreak trace mean scores indicates the signal is real but distributions overlap, requiring threshold tuning per deployment context.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 9: False Positive Analysis Summary\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"FPR (default)\", \"Precision@80%Recall\", \"Precision@60%Recall\", \"AUROC\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Static LR\", \"0.18\", \"0.67\", \"0.78\", \"0.79\"]\n          - [\"Temporal LR W=15\", \"0.22\", \"0.71\", \"0.82\", \"0.82\"]\n          - [\"GRU (multi-layer)\", \"0.15\", \"0.74\", \"0.86\", \"0.85\"]\n          - [\"BOCPD\", \"0.12\", \"0.68\", \"0.79\", \"0.81\"]\n      - name: \"Table 9b: Operating Point Analysis (Temporal LR)\"\n        columns: [\"Target FPR\", \"Threshold\", \"Coverage\", \"Precision\", \"F1\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"5%\", \"0.65\", \"0.38\", \"0.92\", \"0.54\"]\n          - [\"10%\", \"0.58\", \"0.52\", \"0.85\", \"0.64\"]\n          - [\"15%\", \"0.51\", \"0.63\", \"0.79\", \"0.70\"]\n          - [\"20%\", \"0.44\", \"0.71\", \"0.73\", \"0.72\"]\n          - [\"25%\", \"0.38\", \"0.76\", \"0.68\", \"0.72\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 9a: Precision-recall curves for all methods. GRU dominates in the high-precision region; temporal LR dominates in the high-recall region.\"\n      - \"Fig 9b: Probe score distribution (kernel density) for safe vs jailbreak traces. Overlap region highlighted, showing why FPR is non-trivial.\"\n      - \"Fig 9c: ROC curves with operating points annotated.\"\n    analysis: \"The FPR analysis reveals that deployment requires explicit threshold tuning — no method achieves both high coverage and low FPR at a single operating point. The best tradeoff depends on deployment context: high-security environments (prefer low FPR) should use BOCPD or high-threshold temporal LR; broad monitoring (prefer high coverage) can tolerate 20% FPR. For the paper, this adds a critical section that addresses reviewer concern about deployment utility. The key message: 'Usable operating points exist (e.g., 85% precision at 52% coverage, or 73% precision at 71% coverage), but require threshold calibration on a per-deployment basis.' This is honest about limitations while showing the method has practical value.\"\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"ec1257e8-db47-4220-9b31-3f50b7783d8d","total_cost_usd":0.44146575000000005,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12133,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":14101,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12133,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":14101,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12133,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12133},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":7094,"outputTokens":17,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.007179,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":14101,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":12133,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.43428675000000005,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"305135bd-b8c8-481b-b824-36537f786f01"}
